      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_e3ccaccc24aba9553c4246910b3d999335f7a2a5202da532905cd94290367f8c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: a truncated Perron integral for the cubic moment is shifted left, crossing an order-seven pole at w=1 and a simple pole at w=3/4. These yield respectively x times a degree-six logarithmic polynomial and a residue multiple of x^(3/4); a power choice of T balances the stated errors under a vertical-growth hypothesis. Interpretation: pole order becomes logarithmic degree, pole location becomes an asymptotic power, and vertical growth determines whether the contour can expose the secondary term with a smaller error. Boundary: continuation, both pole statements, and the growth bound are inputs; failure of the growth bound would defeat this error estimate without undoing the residue computation. Uncertainty: the r inequality, T choice, exponents, and constant need scan verification.",
